%[text] # Kohn-Sham DFT with the PBE Functional
%[text] This live script is a plain-text adaptation of `Lecture10_Kohn_Sham_DFT.mlx`. It keeps the same real-space radial Kohn-Sham solver for closed-shell atoms, but replaces the local-density approximation with the generalized-gradient Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) exchange-correlation functional.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Contents"}
%%
%[text] ## Why Replace LDA by PBE
%[text] In the original lecture the exchange-correlation term depends only on the local density $\\rho(r) $. That local-density approximation is simple and useful, but atomic densities vary rapidly near the nucleus and in the density tail, so gradient corrections are often important. In the PBE generalized-gradient approximation the exchange-correlation energy depends on both the density and its radial derivative,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ E\_{xc}\[\n\\rho\] = \\int \n\\rho(\\mathbf{r}) \\epsilon\_{xc}(\n\\rho,\n\\nabla \n\\rho) d\\mathbf{r}. $
%[text] For a spherically symmetric density the corresponding exchange-correlation potential becomes
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ V\_{xc}(r) = \\frac{\\delta E\_{xc}\[\\rho\]}{\\delta \\rho(\\mathbf{r})}=\\frac{\\partial (\n\\rho \\epsilon\_{xc})}{\\partial \n\\rho} - \\frac{1}{r^2}\\frac{d}{dr}\\left\[r^2 \\frac{\\partial (\n\\rho \\epsilon\_{xc})}{\\partial (\\partial \n\\rho/\\partial r)}\n\\right\]. $
%[text] The numerical solver below evaluates this radial functional derivative directly on the finite-difference grid.
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ## Example Calculation
%[text] Choose an atomic number and a radial grid, then run the self-consistent PBE calculation. Compared with the original LDA lecture, a small amount of density mixing is used because GGA functionals are usually less forgiving numerically.
Z = 2;
R_MAX = 40;
Nd = 2000;
FDn = 4;
tol = 1e-5;
S_DFT = SCF(init_DFT(Z,R_MAX,Nd,tol,FDn)); %[output:2b05ad2b]
disp("Ground-state energy decomposition (Hartree units)") %[output:3a40cfdd]
disp(table(S_DFT.Ek,S_DFT.Eext,S_DFT.Eh,S_DFT.Ex,S_DFT.Ec,S_DFT.Etot, ... %[output:group:54a121c6] %[output:21569d37]
    'VariableNames',["Kinetic","External","Hartree","Exchange","Correlation","Total"])) %[output:group:54a121c6] %[output:21569d37]
%%
%[text] ## Radial Density and Effective Potential
%[text] The first panel shows the radial probability distribution $ 4 \\pi r^2 \n\\rho(r) $. The second panel shows the converged effective potential that enters the radial Kohn-Sham Hamiltonian.
tiledlayout(1,2) %[output:8782eea6]
nexttile %[output:8782eea6]
plot([0;S_DFT.r],[0;4*pi*S_DFT.rho.*S_DFT.r.^2],'LineWidth',1.5) %[output:8782eea6]
xlabel('r (Bohr)') %[output:8782eea6]
ylabel('4\pi r^2 \rho(r)') %[output:8782eea6]
title('Radial Electron Density') %[output:8782eea6]
grid on %[output:8782eea6]
xlim([0 Z+4]) %[output:8782eea6]
if Z==1 
    hold on
    syms rh
    fplot(4*rh^2*exp(-2*rh))
    xline(1)
    xlim([0 6])
    legend("Converged KS-DFT","Hydrogen exact",'Interpreter','latex')
    hold off
end
load HFrho
if Z==2
    hold on %[output:8782eea6]
    plot(HFrho.x,HFrho.y); %[output:8782eea6]
    legend("KS-DFT ","HF ",'Interpreter','latex') %[output:8782eea6]
else 
    legend("KS-DFT $\rho$",'Interpreter','latex')
    hold off;
end
nexttile %[output:8782eea6]
plot(S_DFT.r,S_DFT.Veff,'LineWidth',1.5) %[output:8782eea6]
xlabel('r (Bohr)') %[output:8782eea6]
ylabel('V_{eff}(r)') %[output:8782eea6]
title('Effective (Kohn-Sham) Potential') %[output:8782eea6]
grid on %[output:8782eea6]
xlim([0.2 5]) %[output:8782eea6]
